## Where to look next

### Instructions the runs were served

Each run was served the blueprint as it was at one commit. In a clone of your blueprint repo, `git show <commit>:<path>` prints a file as that run saw it. Routines are in `routines/<name>.md`, skills in `skills/<name>/SKILL.md` and rules in `rules/<name>.mdc`; a run's own file lists the skills and rules it loaded.

- `ee8f142` (factory acme, 2 runs)
  - `git -C <your blueprint clone> show ee8f142:routines/pr-review.md`

- `b4c5d6e` (factory acme, 1 run)
  - `git -C <your blueprint clone> show b4c5d6e:routines/pr-review.md`

If your blueprint lives in a subfolder of its repo, put that folder in front of the path.

### Pull requests

The review comments on these are often the best sign of what went wrong.

- https://github.com/acme/app/pull/413 (run_01m54cx6ng1000000000000000, succeeded)
- https://github.com/acme/app/pull/412 (run_01m54cx6ng0000000000000001, succeeded)
- https://github.com/acme/app/pull/380 (run_01m3veg5800000000000000005, succeeded)

### Transcripts

No run points at a file on this machine. Some may point at a session URL or ID instead; that's the `session` of each run.

## Fields you can't fully trust

Agents write the intents, summaries, statuses, friction notes, links and session pointers, on a best-effort basis, so they can be missing, wrong or vague. A run that says `succeeded` may not have. Fabrica writes the times, the blueprint commits, the factory and the loads, so those are reliable, and so are its own refusals.
